WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.097 --> 00:00:02.390
- "This episode of "The Good
Doctor" is a made up story

00:00:02.390 --> 00:00:04.300
about a real battle still being fought.

00:00:04.300 --> 00:00:06.100
Honor the heroes, doctors, nurses,

00:00:06.100 --> 00:00:07.490
and other frontline workers,

00:00:07.490 --> 00:00:09.120
many of whom who have given their lives.

00:00:09.120 --> 00:00:10.540
Do your part, wear a mask."

00:00:10.540 --> 00:00:11.383
Great message.

00:00:13.260 --> 00:00:16.010
(woman coughing)

00:00:17.070 --> 00:00:19.350
I love the way they're
showing the viral particles.

00:00:19.350 --> 00:00:21.070
They're just spreading out everywhere.

00:00:21.070 --> 00:00:22.640
That absolutely does happen.

00:00:22.640 --> 00:00:24.210
That's why it's really important to dab

00:00:24.210 --> 00:00:26.420
when you cough to try
and limit that spread.

00:00:26.420 --> 00:00:29.460
We've seen it actually
spray six feet away from you

00:00:29.460 --> 00:00:30.810
when you cough or sneeze.

00:00:30.810 --> 00:00:32.530
And germs have hang time.

00:00:32.530 --> 00:00:33.970
So you can walk into an empty room

00:00:33.970 --> 00:00:37.313
that someone just sneezed it
and be exposed to those germs.

00:00:38.490 --> 00:00:40.467
I don't know why I did that.

00:00:40.467 --> 00:00:43.217
(dramatic music)

00:00:46.626 --> 00:00:47.740
- And there you go.

00:00:47.740 --> 00:00:50.140
- This is such a good
example of how germs spread,

00:00:50.140 --> 00:00:53.150
droplets, meaning someone
coughs or sneezes or speaks.

00:00:53.150 --> 00:00:55.430
Contact, meaning they
coughed on their hands

00:00:55.430 --> 00:00:57.540
and they touch something,
they give it to somebody else.

00:00:57.540 --> 00:01:00.300
When we talk about disinfecting
your hands, wearing a mask,

00:01:00.300 --> 00:01:02.600
we're talking about
decreasing droplet spread

00:01:02.600 --> 00:01:03.433
and contact spread.

00:01:03.433 --> 00:01:05.516
- Have you seen my ID badge?
- No.

00:01:05.516 --> 00:01:07.320
- I had it last night,

00:01:07.320 --> 00:01:09.530
but maybe it fell out of my bag.

00:01:09.530 --> 00:01:12.200
- I lose my ID badge all the time.

00:01:12.200 --> 00:01:14.750
Like not permanently lose
it, but have it in my car,

00:01:14.750 --> 00:01:17.420
in my trunk, in my suitcase, in my home.

00:01:17.420 --> 00:01:18.920
Sometimes Bear steals it.

00:01:18.920 --> 00:01:19.753
Sorry, Bear.

00:01:19.753 --> 00:01:21.250
- Your temperature's almost 101.

00:01:21.250 --> 00:01:22.930
Your other symptoms?

00:01:22.930 --> 00:01:25.130
- First, it was a sore throat.

00:01:25.130 --> 00:01:26.746
Then my body started aching.

00:01:26.746 --> 00:01:28.900
(coughing)

00:01:28.900 --> 00:01:30.813
And then there's this cough.

00:01:30.813 --> 00:01:32.670
I even had my flu shot.

00:01:32.670 --> 00:01:35.520
- Unfortunately, influenza
is constantly mutating.

00:01:35.520 --> 00:01:37.090
The shot always lags behind.

00:01:37.090 --> 00:01:38.620
- Both very accurate statements.

00:01:38.620 --> 00:01:40.790
In fact, during this
timeframe in February,

00:01:40.790 --> 00:01:42.840
I was working in my primary care office.

00:01:42.840 --> 00:01:44.930
We knew COVID was around,

00:01:44.930 --> 00:01:47.160
but we didn't think it was everywhere yet.

00:01:47.160 --> 00:01:49.080
So a lot of patients were
coming to my practice

00:01:49.080 --> 00:01:51.545
with upper respiratory viral
symptoms, just like this.

00:01:51.545 --> 00:01:53.420
Fever, cough, muscle aches

00:01:53.420 --> 00:01:55.870
and we thought they
potentially had influenza.

00:01:55.870 --> 00:01:58.360
We gave them antivirals for influenza.

00:01:58.360 --> 00:02:00.130
The reality is even if
you had your flu shot,

00:02:00.130 --> 00:02:03.090
you can still get the flu, but
your case should be milder.

00:02:03.090 --> 00:02:05.940
- My daughter is very worried
that I might have this virus

00:02:05.940 --> 00:02:08.210
from China, what's it called?

00:02:08.210 --> 00:02:09.972
- Have you been to China
or been around anyone

00:02:09.972 --> 00:02:11.440
who's been to China recently?

00:02:11.440 --> 00:02:13.020
- Those are the questions we were asking.

00:02:13.020 --> 00:02:14.370
At that time, I think we were referring

00:02:14.370 --> 00:02:16.710
it to NCOV-19, right?

00:02:16.710 --> 00:02:17.971
Was it NCOV-19?

00:02:17.971 --> 00:02:20.721
(woman coughing)

00:02:22.110 --> 00:02:24.310
- The virus has damaged
your mother's lungs.

00:02:24.310 --> 00:02:26.320
Her body is working harder
and harder to breathe,

00:02:26.320 --> 00:02:28.920
but her blood oxygen
levels continue to drop.

00:02:28.920 --> 00:02:30.773
- She definitely has corona?

00:02:33.980 --> 00:02:35.869
- We're doing everything
we can to help her.

00:02:35.869 --> 00:02:38.702
(dramatic music)

00:02:44.860 --> 00:02:46.830
- See that patient was actually

00:02:46.830 --> 00:02:48.690
in a negative pressure air room.

00:02:48.690 --> 00:02:50.480
They were isolated from other people

00:02:50.480 --> 00:02:53.560
because at that point we were
deciding whether COVID-19

00:02:53.560 --> 00:02:56.500
was only needed to maintain
a droplet precaution

00:02:56.500 --> 00:02:57.880
where you just cover your face

00:02:57.880 --> 00:02:59.500
and the patient's face with a mask,

00:02:59.500 --> 00:03:01.266
or do we need negative pressure room?

00:03:01.266 --> 00:03:03.880
Because COVID was spread
to airborne transmission

00:03:03.880 --> 00:03:06.500
where the virus just lived
in aerosolized particles

00:03:06.500 --> 00:03:07.850
in the air all the time.

00:03:07.850 --> 00:03:09.570
And what we came to realize is

00:03:09.570 --> 00:03:11.460
it's more of a droplet spread,

00:03:11.460 --> 00:03:13.940
but there are some instances
where it can be spread

00:03:13.940 --> 00:03:15.210
through aerosolized particles,

00:03:15.210 --> 00:03:18.450
especially during medical
procedures like intubations.

00:03:18.450 --> 00:03:19.563
- You have COVID.

00:03:21.082 --> 00:03:23.510
(man sighs)

00:03:23.510 --> 00:03:25.320
- Okay, now what?

00:03:25.320 --> 00:03:27.680
- Now you go home,.

00:03:27.680 --> 00:03:28.722
- That's it?
- That's it.

00:03:28.722 --> 00:03:29.940
There's no treatment.

00:03:29.940 --> 00:03:30.880
Stay away from people.

00:03:30.880 --> 00:03:33.330
Come back if you have
trouble breathing, goodbye.

00:03:34.340 --> 00:03:36.280
- I forget 'cause I don't
watch this show regularly

00:03:36.280 --> 00:03:37.120
how funny Shaun is.

00:03:37.120 --> 00:03:38.564
COVID-19 is a viral illness.

00:03:38.564 --> 00:03:41.870
So if your oxygen levels
are not dropping drastically

00:03:41.870 --> 00:03:44.540
and you just kind of feel lousy
and you have a mild fever,

00:03:44.540 --> 00:03:46.870
your body is the one that
has to fight this off.

00:03:46.870 --> 00:03:48.530
Like there's very little
that we can actually do

00:03:48.530 --> 00:03:50.000
in the hospital systems.

00:03:50.000 --> 00:03:51.740
And by keeping you in the hospital,

00:03:51.740 --> 00:03:53.936
we're actually increasing the
risk to healthcare providers

00:03:53.936 --> 00:03:55.120
and other patients.

00:03:55.120 --> 00:03:57.703
- 25 tests per day?

00:03:58.640 --> 00:04:00.433
No, just give me one of tomorrows.

00:04:01.670 --> 00:04:03.260
Hello?

00:04:03.260 --> 00:04:04.530
County health department,
I don't have time for them

00:04:04.530 --> 00:04:06.030
And apparently they
don't have time for me.

00:04:06.030 --> 00:04:08.140
- I remember in March I had a patient

00:04:08.140 --> 00:04:10.210
who I suspected to have COVID-19

00:04:10.210 --> 00:04:11.910
but I didn't have a test available.

00:04:11.910 --> 00:04:13.170
So I was calling the department of health,

00:04:13.170 --> 00:04:14.420
trying to secure a test for them.

00:04:14.420 --> 00:04:15.690
Eventually getting them a test,

00:04:15.690 --> 00:04:17.679
finding out that they
were in fact positive.

00:04:17.679 --> 00:04:20.480
Luckily everyone was
wearing a mask at that point

00:04:20.480 --> 00:04:22.550
so we decrease the spread,
we cleaned the room,

00:04:22.550 --> 00:04:23.960
sent them out the back door,

00:04:23.960 --> 00:04:26.550
tried to do our best to limit
the spread of this virus.

00:04:26.550 --> 00:04:28.870
There's just a lot of things
we didn't know in the beginning

00:04:28.870 --> 00:04:30.110
that we know now.

00:04:30.110 --> 00:04:31.640
- If I'm sick is my baby sick?

00:04:31.640 --> 00:04:33.740
- This disease doesn't really
seem to affect children

00:04:33.740 --> 00:04:36.140
and babies like it does adults.

00:04:36.140 --> 00:04:37.340
Should we admit her?

00:04:37.340 --> 00:04:38.890
- She's probably safer at home.

00:04:40.790 --> 00:04:43.800
- What's at home, do you
have a support system?

00:04:43.800 --> 00:04:45.340
Can anyone come help you-
- Good question.

00:04:45.340 --> 00:04:47.190
- In case you get worse?

00:04:47.190 --> 00:04:50.099
- It's just me and the little one.

00:04:50.099 --> 00:04:53.420
- Good doctors always
ask questions like this.

00:04:53.420 --> 00:04:54.607
If I'm sending a patient home

00:04:54.607 --> 00:04:56.720
that I'm sort of questionable on

00:04:56.720 --> 00:04:58.270
and let's say they had a concussion,

00:04:58.270 --> 00:04:59.840
I always want to make
sure that there someone

00:04:59.840 --> 00:05:00.790
to keep an eye on them.

00:05:00.790 --> 00:05:02.270
That if they become unconscious,

00:05:02.270 --> 00:05:04.570
they have someone to call for
help and to check on them.

00:05:04.570 --> 00:05:06.430
- If I come home, do you
want me living with you?

00:05:06.430 --> 00:05:08.090
- [Woman] Of course, that's the point.

00:05:08.090 --> 00:05:10.060
- Every night after 12
hours of COVID patients

00:05:10.060 --> 00:05:12.780
to hang out with you and
Kellan and his asthma?

00:05:12.780 --> 00:05:13.890
- Interestingly enough,

00:05:13.890 --> 00:05:16.130
we've seen conflicting
reports on how COVID-19

00:05:16.130 --> 00:05:17.460
affects those with asthma.

00:05:17.460 --> 00:05:19.350
Now, if it's just asthma alone,

00:05:19.350 --> 00:05:22.470
it doesn't seem to be as
bad as we expected it to be.

00:05:22.470 --> 00:05:25.540
But those who are asthmatic
and have other comorbidities

00:05:25.540 --> 00:05:27.360
like heart disease, obesity,

00:05:27.360 --> 00:05:29.430
those are the patients that
seem to be experiencing

00:05:29.430 --> 00:05:31.603
worse symptoms and worse outcomes.

00:05:31.603 --> 00:05:32.810
- We do everything we can.

00:05:32.810 --> 00:05:35.450
We hammer at official channels

00:05:35.450 --> 00:05:37.960
and every manufacturer's
rep that we deal with

00:05:37.960 --> 00:05:40.300
and see what companies have if any have-

00:05:40.300 --> 00:05:42.183
- [Woman] Nuts or no nuts?

00:05:46.081 --> 00:05:50.750
- I'm sorry, I'm in the middle
of a meeting with the board.

00:05:50.750 --> 00:05:52.280
- Hey, everyone!

00:05:52.280 --> 00:05:54.340
I'm just baking banana bread.

00:05:54.340 --> 00:05:58.550
- One of the most search terms
in height of this pandemic

00:05:58.550 --> 00:06:00.790
was how to make sourdough bread.

00:06:00.790 --> 00:06:02.790
Go figure, I've never
made sourdough bread,

00:06:02.790 --> 00:06:03.690
I've never Googled it.

00:06:03.690 --> 00:06:05.236
I don't know who's sitting
at home Googling it,

00:06:05.236 --> 00:06:07.160
but apparently sourdough bread

00:06:07.160 --> 00:06:09.840
was the thing everyone was
making, not banana bread.

00:06:09.840 --> 00:06:13.363
- Your husband's blood oxygen
level is dangerously low.

00:06:14.670 --> 00:06:16.390
- That, that can't be.

00:06:16.390 --> 00:06:18.380
- So one of the hallmark
symptoms of COVID-19

00:06:18.380 --> 00:06:21.200
is decreased oxygen
saturation in your blood

00:06:21.200 --> 00:06:23.240
where you're actually
not getting enough oxygen

00:06:23.240 --> 00:06:25.660
from usually the edema or the scarring

00:06:25.660 --> 00:06:27.510
that's happening in your lungs

00:06:27.510 --> 00:06:30.920
as a result of the inflammation
that occurs with this virus.

00:06:30.920 --> 00:06:33.300
One of the dangerous parts of COVID-19 is

00:06:33.300 --> 00:06:35.000
not only are you infected with this virus

00:06:35.000 --> 00:06:36.010
that you're fighting off,

00:06:36.010 --> 00:06:39.010
but some people have an
overreaction or a cytokine storm

00:06:39.010 --> 00:06:41.290
where all these inflammatory
cells come to the lungs.

00:06:41.290 --> 00:06:42.560
It actually causes a bigger problem

00:06:42.560 --> 00:06:44.580
where you can't get enough oxygen going

00:06:44.580 --> 00:06:46.192
and being delivered to
the rest of your body,

00:06:46.192 --> 00:06:48.570
your vital organs, your
liver, your kidneys,

00:06:48.570 --> 00:06:49.740
your brain and so forth.

00:06:49.740 --> 00:06:51.820
Our solution to that was to ventilate,

00:06:51.820 --> 00:06:53.810
meaning intubate all these patients.

00:06:53.810 --> 00:06:55.990
And as we did that more and more often

00:06:55.990 --> 00:06:58.260
without treating them
with proper medications

00:06:58.260 --> 00:07:00.370
like we have now and we
know which ones work,

00:07:00.370 --> 00:07:03.410
patients actually got worse on ventilators

00:07:03.410 --> 00:07:05.290
because we were over ventilating people.

00:07:05.290 --> 00:07:07.370
And it's not because we're stupid

00:07:07.370 --> 00:07:09.490
or we were intentionally harming people,

00:07:09.490 --> 00:07:11.580
but it was simply because
it's a novel virus

00:07:11.580 --> 00:07:13.050
and we did not have guidelines

00:07:13.050 --> 00:07:15.290
on how to best treat patients with it.

00:07:15.290 --> 00:07:17.320
We were learning about it on the go.

00:07:17.320 --> 00:07:18.750
- We have a surplus of EKG leads?

00:07:18.750 --> 00:07:20.060
- No, we've got a shortage,

00:07:20.060 --> 00:07:21.530
but that's a problem that can wait.

00:07:21.530 --> 00:07:23.203
- The PBE situation was really dire

00:07:23.203 --> 00:07:24.640
in the beginning of this pandemic

00:07:24.640 --> 00:07:26.370
because China was hit first

00:07:26.370 --> 00:07:29.200
and a lot of our manufacturing
facilities are in China.

00:07:29.200 --> 00:07:30.380
And because of that,

00:07:30.380 --> 00:07:32.980
they actually redirected
all their shipments

00:07:32.980 --> 00:07:35.310
to their country because they needed them.

00:07:35.310 --> 00:07:38.530
And as a result, we're facing
a lot of shortages for gloves,

00:07:38.530 --> 00:07:42.010
for EKG leads, for swabs, for test kits,

00:07:42.010 --> 00:07:45.880
antibacterial wipes, the
vials, for injections.

00:07:45.880 --> 00:07:48.933
The wiring and the tubing for ventilators.

00:07:48.933 --> 00:07:51.190
There were so many shortages
that were happening

00:07:51.190 --> 00:07:53.890
simultaneously, it was
a truly dire situation,

00:07:53.890 --> 00:07:56.624
especially here in the New York City area.

00:07:56.624 --> 00:07:59.041
(soft music)

00:08:00.260 --> 00:08:02.790
Him rubbing his ears like
that is from the mask.

00:08:02.790 --> 00:08:04.370
Oh my God, in the beginning,

00:08:04.370 --> 00:08:07.070
just wearing the mask for countless hours.

00:08:07.070 --> 00:08:08.420
My ears heard so much.

00:08:08.420 --> 00:08:10.850
I just remember being so sore,

00:08:10.850 --> 00:08:13.610
but as we learn to use those
little headpieces in the back

00:08:13.610 --> 00:08:16.020
where you could actually
have the mask stay on there

00:08:16.020 --> 00:08:17.160
as opposed to on your ears,

00:08:17.160 --> 00:08:19.190
it made the world of a difference.

00:08:19.190 --> 00:08:22.700
And for those of you who
haven't ever worn an N95 mask,

00:08:22.700 --> 00:08:25.420
they're uncomfortable because
they offer more protection.

00:08:25.420 --> 00:08:27.810
They're more form fitting, you
have to get them fit tested,

00:08:27.810 --> 00:08:29.490
but they also put a lot
of pressure on your face.

00:08:29.490 --> 00:08:30.480
They leave marks.

00:08:30.480 --> 00:08:31.520
That's why all those pictures

00:08:31.520 --> 00:08:32.780
were making the rounds of nurses,

00:08:32.780 --> 00:08:35.071
doctors who were having
bruising in that area

00:08:35.071 --> 00:08:37.380
from just wearing them day in and day out.

00:08:37.380 --> 00:08:39.410
And a lot of times they
were reusing equipment

00:08:39.410 --> 00:08:40.920
that was meant to be disposable.

00:08:40.920 --> 00:08:42.220
- You work in a hospital.

00:08:42.220 --> 00:08:43.790
- Yes.

00:08:43.790 --> 00:08:46.323
- Could you wait for the next elevator?

00:08:51.030 --> 00:08:52.183
I appreciate you.

00:08:54.515 --> 00:08:55.420
(laughing)

00:08:55.420 --> 00:08:56.670
- You know, it's funny.

00:08:56.670 --> 00:08:59.020
In New York, if you ever
wore your scrubs out

00:08:59.020 --> 00:08:59.853
during that period,

00:08:59.853 --> 00:09:02.660
everyone wanted to say
thank you and was grateful,

00:09:02.660 --> 00:09:03.900
and that's amazing.

00:09:03.900 --> 00:09:04.980
But at the same time,

00:09:04.980 --> 00:09:07.000
everyone wanted to keep their distance.

00:09:07.000 --> 00:09:09.140
Rightfully so, I understand
you don't want to put yourself

00:09:09.140 --> 00:09:11.380
or your family members at
risk, but it didn't feel good

00:09:11.380 --> 00:09:13.100
that you were being alienated like that.

00:09:13.100 --> 00:09:14.300
So it's part of the process.

00:09:14.300 --> 00:09:16.301
- My mother is in there all alone,

00:09:16.301 --> 00:09:17.532
hooked up to a machine.

00:09:17.532 --> 00:09:20.770
I just need to see her and tell her

00:09:22.330 --> 00:09:23.163
that I love her.

00:09:23.163 --> 00:09:26.053
- There's a human toll that
it takes on family members

00:09:26.053 --> 00:09:28.130
not being able to see your family member

00:09:28.130 --> 00:09:30.640
and if you're the one ill not
seeing your family members.

00:09:30.640 --> 00:09:33.280
I will also venture to say,

00:09:33.280 --> 00:09:34.720
and I don't have evidence for this,

00:09:34.720 --> 00:09:38.200
but I will say that
hospital errors do go up

00:09:38.200 --> 00:09:40.377
if you do not have family members present

00:09:40.377 --> 00:09:42.040
and advocating on your behalf,

00:09:42.040 --> 00:09:43.440
especially if you're unconscious.

00:09:43.440 --> 00:09:44.750
Now, what we've learned from that

00:09:44.750 --> 00:09:47.010
is we're trying to keep
families more updated,

00:09:47.010 --> 00:09:48.360
trying to keep them involved.

00:09:48.360 --> 00:09:51.020
If they're not there at the
bedside, through virtual care,

00:09:51.020 --> 00:09:53.194
giving them scheduled regular phone calls,

00:09:53.194 --> 00:09:55.760
because we realize that when
we're in better communication

00:09:55.760 --> 00:09:57.760
with the family, we
actually get better outcomes

00:09:57.760 --> 00:09:58.764
for our patients.

00:09:58.764 --> 00:10:02.070
(machine flatlining)

00:10:02.070 --> 00:10:03.170
- [Nurse] Pushing epi.

00:10:07.910 --> 00:10:09.650
- Why is no one doing chest compressions?

00:10:09.650 --> 00:10:10.483
I don't understand.

00:10:10.483 --> 00:10:12.400
Do some chest compressions.
- 120 Joules, clear.

00:10:12.400 --> 00:10:14.757
- Do some chest compressions.

00:10:14.757 --> 00:10:15.757
- [Nurse] Refib now.

00:10:18.260 --> 00:10:19.870
- Did you see those
horrible chest compressions?

00:10:19.870 --> 00:10:22.010
She just put her hands on the
chest, didn't even compress.

00:10:22.010 --> 00:10:25.050
- 200 Joules, clear.

00:10:25.050 --> 00:10:26.630
- Okay, I don't know why,

00:10:26.630 --> 00:10:29.600
these people have high end experts on set.

00:10:29.600 --> 00:10:31.570
You start chest compressions

00:10:31.570 --> 00:10:33.470
and then you use a defibrillation.

00:10:33.470 --> 00:10:35.380
I have heard in the
beginning of the pandemic,

00:10:35.380 --> 00:10:37.290
'cause I wasn't in the hospitals.

00:10:37.290 --> 00:10:39.310
I was seeing patients, outpatient,

00:10:39.310 --> 00:10:41.620
that they were decreasing the
amount of chest compressions

00:10:41.620 --> 00:10:42.830
being done because they were worried about

00:10:42.830 --> 00:10:44.050
aerosolizing the virus.

00:10:44.050 --> 00:10:45.980
I actually just looked
this up from the Red Cross,

00:10:45.980 --> 00:10:47.176
performing chest compressions

00:10:47.176 --> 00:10:49.950
while wearing proper medical
protective equipment,

00:10:49.950 --> 00:10:53.250
N95 respirators or how they
have the whole body suit on,

00:10:53.250 --> 00:10:54.777
still means you should
do chest compressions,

00:10:54.777 --> 00:10:56.230
like do them, do them, do them.

00:10:56.230 --> 00:10:57.390
I don't know why they weren't doing them.

00:10:57.390 --> 00:10:58.680
I was trying to make excuses for them.

00:10:58.680 --> 00:11:00.010
They should have been
doing chest compressions

00:11:00.010 --> 00:11:00.843
from the start.

00:11:00.843 --> 00:11:02.255
- Any cough?
(thermometer beeping)

00:11:02.255 --> 00:11:03.760
- Any fever?
(thermometer beeping)

00:11:03.760 --> 00:11:05.250
- No.
- Any shortness of breath?

00:11:05.250 --> 00:11:06.740
- Sore throat, loss of smell?

00:11:06.740 --> 00:11:07.610
- No, no, none of that.

00:11:07.610 --> 00:11:11.550
Just searing abdominal pain and the runs.

00:11:11.550 --> 00:11:14.410
I'm pretty sure it's my
diverticulitis acting up.

00:11:14.410 --> 00:11:16.993
My roommate is deeply
into pandemic baking.

00:11:19.080 --> 00:11:21.360
I've avoided coming in, I
don't want to catch COVID.

00:11:21.360 --> 00:11:23.620
- So one of the biggest problems we faced

00:11:23.620 --> 00:11:25.350
during this pandemic was patients

00:11:25.350 --> 00:11:26.890
not coming into the hospitals.

00:11:26.890 --> 00:11:29.460
And in the beginning, when
we were really overwhelmed,

00:11:29.460 --> 00:11:30.650
that made a lot of sense.

00:11:30.650 --> 00:11:33.670
But as we became safer
for patients to come in,

00:11:33.670 --> 00:11:35.325
we had different policies in place

00:11:35.325 --> 00:11:36.796
in order to decrease spread,

00:11:36.796 --> 00:11:39.140
our testing improved,
our capacity improved,

00:11:39.140 --> 00:11:40.280
our PPE improved.

00:11:40.280 --> 00:11:41.880
We wanted patients to come back

00:11:41.880 --> 00:11:44.240
because a lot of times
when patients delay care,

00:11:44.240 --> 00:11:46.710
their diseases and
conditions get more advanced

00:11:46.710 --> 00:11:48.870
and as a result become
more difficult to treat.

00:11:48.870 --> 00:11:51.810
Patients who are having
some signs of heart attacks

00:11:51.810 --> 00:11:54.100
were coming in much later and as a result,

00:11:54.100 --> 00:11:57.010
some of our mainstay treatments
weren't working as well.

00:11:57.010 --> 00:11:58.550
And the recoveries took longer.

00:11:58.550 --> 00:12:01.270
One of my main messages over
the last two, three months

00:12:01.270 --> 00:12:03.250
has been to encourage patients

00:12:03.250 --> 00:12:06.530
to call their primary care
office, to call hospitals,

00:12:06.530 --> 00:12:08.600
to come in for the symptoms
that are bothering them

00:12:08.600 --> 00:12:09.880
and not wait.

00:12:09.880 --> 00:12:12.357
- He's pink, active and breathing great.

00:12:12.357 --> 00:12:14.478
(baby crying)

00:12:14.478 --> 00:12:17.311
(monitor beeping)

00:12:21.480 --> 00:12:23.440
- In the midst of our
research with COVID-19,

00:12:23.440 --> 00:12:25.440
we actually found that
there was a limited spread

00:12:25.440 --> 00:12:28.760
for mother to fetus of COVID-19
meaning that the mother

00:12:28.760 --> 00:12:30.810
could be testing positive for COVID-19.

00:12:30.810 --> 00:12:32.460
The baby was usually safe.

00:12:32.460 --> 00:12:34.010
Now that doesn't mean there was no spread.

00:12:34.010 --> 00:12:36.220
There were some case
reports of it happening.

00:12:36.220 --> 00:12:37.600
But for the large majority,

00:12:37.600 --> 00:12:39.280
we did not want mothers to panic

00:12:39.280 --> 00:12:40.800
because it wasn't happening.

00:12:40.800 --> 00:12:43.030
And children in general
seem to fare better

00:12:43.030 --> 00:12:44.490
with this virus anyway.

00:12:44.490 --> 00:12:47.340
The numbers of children
below the age of 10 dying

00:12:47.340 --> 00:12:50.020
with COVID-19 were spectacularly low.

00:12:50.020 --> 00:12:51.840
That doesn't mean it's
completely benign for them

00:12:51.840 --> 00:12:54.280
'cause they could also spread
this virus and be vectors.

00:12:54.280 --> 00:12:56.364
But it is reassuring for mothers,

00:12:56.364 --> 00:12:58.429
especially with the levels of anxiety

00:12:58.429 --> 00:13:00.423
that they were facing for themselves.

00:13:01.600 --> 00:13:04.183
(somber music)

00:13:06.700 --> 00:13:09.380
Please don't put your masks on the floor

00:13:09.380 --> 00:13:11.450
and then put them back on your face.

00:13:11.450 --> 00:13:13.490
Shaun, you're a doctor buddy.

00:13:13.490 --> 00:13:15.160
Don't put it on the floor.

00:13:15.160 --> 00:13:17.290
I talked about germs having hang time,

00:13:17.290 --> 00:13:19.660
about the hanging out in the air,

00:13:19.660 --> 00:13:21.670
but folks, after they hang out in the air

00:13:21.670 --> 00:13:24.005
for those minutes or
hours, where do they go?

00:13:24.005 --> 00:13:25.760
Right on the floor,

00:13:25.760 --> 00:13:28.340
especially if you've been
walking around a hospital setting

00:13:28.340 --> 00:13:30.050
and then you come home with your shoes on,

00:13:30.050 --> 00:13:31.180
guess what's on that mask?

00:13:31.180 --> 00:13:32.830
Man, couldn't they just have me on set

00:13:32.830 --> 00:13:34.133
for one of these things.

00:13:36.620 --> 00:13:39.670
Could of help them make
it so much more accurate.

00:13:39.670 --> 00:13:41.420
My hospital actually created

00:13:41.420 --> 00:13:43.730
a state-of-the-art COVID-19 ward.

00:13:43.730 --> 00:13:45.850
Check it out as I give
you the tour for that

00:13:45.850 --> 00:13:46.960
in a day of my life video,

00:13:46.960 --> 00:13:49.830
or check out all of my
"Good Doctor" reviews

00:13:49.830 --> 00:13:52.510
here in a playlist I made just for you.

00:13:52.510 --> 00:13:54.790
And as always, stay happy and healthy

00:13:54.790 --> 00:13:57.139
and stay alert, not anxious.

00:13:57.139 --> 00:13:58.747
(upbeat music)

